window.AILETMOCK={"n":2,"title":"Mock Test 2","theme":"Amendments and personal liberty","kind":"Full-length paper","blurb":"Amendment power, the Ninth Schedule, internet shutdowns, Section 377, adultery and federalism in constitutional law. Seven more branches of law and contemporary legal GK complete the paper.","minutes":120,"neg":0.25,"sec":["Constitutional Law","Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","International Law and Human Rights","Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK"],"q":[{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Amendment Power","q":"Which decision held that Parliament cannot amend Part III (Fundamental Rights) and applied the doctrine of prospective overruling?","o":["Golak Nath v. State of Punjab (1967)","Indira Nehru Gandhi v. Raj Narain (1975)","Shankari Prasad v. Union of India (1951)","Sajjan Singh v. State of Rajasthan (1965)"],"a":0,"e":"Golak Nath (11 judges, 6:5) held that Article 368 only provides procedure and that Parliament cannot take away fundamental rights. It was overruled in Kesavananda Bharati. Shankari Prasad and Sajjan Singh had earlier upheld Parliament's power to amend Part III."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"DPSP and FR","q":"Which decision stated that the Constitution rests on a balance between Part III and Part IV, and that this harmony is part of the basic structure?","o":["Golak Nath v. State of Punjab (1967)","State of Madras v. Champakam Dorairajan (1951)","I.R. Coelho v. State of Tamil Nadu (2007)","Minerva Mills Ltd. v. Union of India (1980)"],"a":3,"e":"Minerva Mills struck down Sections 4 and 55 of the 42nd Amendment, holding that limited amending power and judicial review are basic features, and that Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles together form the core of the Constitution."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Ninth Schedule","q":"In I.R. Coelho v. State of Tamil Nadu (2007), laws placed in the Ninth Schedule are open to basic structure review if placed after:","o":["28 April 1983","26 January 1950","24 April 1973","3 January 1977"],"a":2,"e":"The nine-judge bench held that Ninth Schedule laws inserted after the Kesavananda Bharati judgment (24 April 1973) can be tested against fundamental rights that form the basic structure (Articles 14, 19 and 21)."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Internet Shutdowns","q":"In Anuradha Bhasin v. Union of India (2020), the Supreme Court held that suspension of internet services:","o":["is valid without any duty to publish the suspension orders at all","is permissible indefinitely if the Government considers it necessary for public order","must be temporary, proportionate and reviewable, and its orders must be published","is outside judicial review, as it relates to national security matters"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that freedom of speech and trade through the internet is protected under Article 19(1)(a) and 19(1)(g), and that indefinite suspension is impermissible; orders are subject to the proportionality test and judicial review."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Section 377","q":"Navjot Singh Johar v. Union of India (2018) decriminalised consensual same-sex conduct between adults, overruling:","o":["Joseph Shine v. Union of India (2018)","Suresh Kumar Koushal v. Naz Foundation (2013)","National Legal Services Authority v. Union of India (2014)","Shakti Vahini v. Union of India (2018)"],"a":1,"e":"A five-judge bench read down Section 377 IPC to the extent that it criminalised consensual acts between adults, holding that Koushal wrongly reversed the Delhi High Court's 2009 Naz Foundation decision."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Adultery","q":"In Joseph Shine v. Union of India (2018), the Supreme Court decriminalised adultery by striking down:","o":["Section 497 of the IPC and Section 198(2) of the CrPC","Section 125 of the CrPC and Section 20 of the DV Act","Section 498A of the IPC and Section 198B of the CrPC","Exception 2 to Section 375 of the IPC and Section 376B"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held Section 497 manifestly arbitrary, treating a wife as her husband's property, and violative of Articles 14, 15 and 21. The BNS does not carry an adultery offence."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Religious Freedom","q":"In Bijoe Emmanuel v. State of Kerala (1986), the Supreme Court held that students belonging to the Jehovah's Witnesses faith could not be compelled to:","o":["attend the morning assembly, as it infringed Article 28(3)","sing the national anthem, since it violated their conscience under Article 25","take an oath of allegiance, as it breached Article 20(3)","salute the national flag, since it infringed their freedom under Article 19"],"a":1,"e":"The Court upheld the students' right not to sing but to stand respectfully, holding that no law obliges anyone to sing the anthem and that expulsion violated Articles 19(1)(a) and 25."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Minority Institutions","q":"In Aligarh Muslim University v. Naresh Agarwal (2024), a 4:3 Constitution Bench overruled which decision on minority character?","o":["St. Stephen's College v. University of Delhi (1992)","S. Azeez Basha v. Union of India (1967)","T.M.A. Pai Foundation v. State of Karnataka (2002)","Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala (1973)"],"a":1,"e":"Azeez Basha had held that an institution established by statute cannot be a minority institution. The 2024 majority held that the court must look to who established and administers the institution; the application of the test to AMU was left to a regular bench."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Federalism","q":"In Mineral Area Development Authority v. SAIL (2024), the nine-judge bench held that:","o":["Parliament alone can regulate mineral rights and their taxation fully","States cannot tax mineral rights, because of Entry 54 of List I at all","royalty on minerals is a tax, so only the Union may levy such a charge","royalty is not a tax, and States can tax mineral rights under List II"],"a":3,"e":"By an 8:1 majority, the Court held that royalty is a contractual payment and not a tax, and that Entry 50 of List II gives States the power to tax mineral rights, subject to any limits Parliament imposes under the MMDR Act."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Federalism","q":"In its October 2024 ruling on the Special Reference concerning 'industrial alcohol', the nine-judge bench held that the power to regulate it lies with:","o":["State legislatures, under Entry 8 of List II","Parliament and States concurrently, under List III","Parliament exclusively, under Entry 52 of List I","local bodies exclusively, under the Eleventh Schedule"],"a":0,"e":"By 8:1, the Court held that States can regulate industrial alcohol as part of their power over 'intoxicating liquors', departing from the narrower view in Synthetics & Chemicals v. State of U.P. (1989)."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Right to Property","q":"In Property Owners Association v. State of Maharashtra (2024), a nine-judge bench held that, under Article 39(b):","o":["not every private resource automatically counts as a 'material resource of the community'","all privately owned resources count as material resources of the community by default","only publicly owned resources can ever be material resources of the community","only agricultural land can count as a material resource of the community by law"],"a":0,"e":"The majority declined to accept the broad view associated with Justice Krishna Iyer in Ranganatha Reddy and Sanjeev Coke, holding that the State may treat some private resources as community resources only after assessing factors such as scarcity and impact on the public good."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Parliament","q":"A joint sitting of both Houses of Parliament under Article 108 is presided over by:","o":["the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha","the Speaker of the Lok Sabha","the Prime Minister as leader of the House","the President of India"],"a":1,"e":"Article 118(4) provides that the Speaker (or, in his absence, the Deputy Speaker) presides at a joint sitting. A joint sitting cannot be called for a Money Bill or a Constitution Amendment Bill."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Ordinances","q":"An Ordinance promulgated under Article 123 ceases to operate, unless disapproved earlier, at the expiration of:","o":["thirty days from the date of promulgation","one year from the date of its first re-promulgation","six weeks from the reassembly of Parliament","six months from the date of promulgation"],"a":2,"e":"D.C. Wadhwa v. State of Bihar (1987) held that repeated re-promulgation of Ordinances without legislative approval is a fraud on the Constitution."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Executive","q":"In Shamsher Singh v. State of Punjab (1974), the Supreme Court held that the President and Governor are:","o":["real executives who may act on their own judgment in all matters of State","bound by ministerial advice on money matters alone, but not on appointments","constitutional heads bound by the aid and advice of the Council of Ministers","free to act in their discretion on every appointment they are asked to make"],"a":2,"e":"A seven-judge bench held that the powers of the President and Governors are exercised on ministerial advice, save in the limited spheres where the Constitution expressly confers discretion."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Electoral Law","q":"Lily Thomas v. Union of India (2013) struck down which provision of the Representation of the People Act, 1951?","o":["Section 8(3), which disqualified persons convicted for two years or more","Section 62(5), which barred persons in prison from voting in any election","Section 29A, which governed registration of political parties with the ECI","Section 8(4), which let convicted legislators continue pending appeal"],"a":3,"e":"After the decision, a legislator convicted of an offence carrying two or more years of imprisonment stands disqualified from the date of conviction, though a stay of conviction can avert the disqualification."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Education","q":"The right to education up to the age of 14 as a fundamental right under Article 21 was held in:","o":["Unni Krishnan J.P. v. State of Andhra Pradesh (1993)","Society for Unaided Private Schools v. Union of India (2012)","T.M.A. Pai Foundation v. State of Karnataka (2002)","Mohini Jain v. State of Karnataka (1992)"],"a":0,"e":"Unni Krishnan limited the right to free education to children up to 14 years, thereby narrowing the broader view in Mohini Jain; the 86th Amendment later added Article 21A."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Retrospective Laws","q":"An Act of 2025 raises the maximum punishment for an offence. X committed the offence in 2023. Under Article 20(1), X can be punished:","o":["up to the new penalty, since he is convicted after the Act is in force","not at all, because the offence itself becomes retrospective in effect","up to the new penalty if the court finds him to be a habitual offender","only to the extent of the penalty in force when he committed the offence"],"a":3,"e":"Article 20(1) bars conviction under an ex post facto law and a penalty greater than that prescribed at the time of the offence. Procedural changes and beneficial laws may apply retrospectively."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Arrest","q":"Under Article 22(2), a person arrested must be produced before the nearest Magistrate within:","o":["24 hours, excluding the time of journey","72 hours, including the time of journey","48 hours, excluding the time of journey","24 hours, including the time of journey"],"a":0,"e":"The 24-hour rule does not apply to persons arrested under preventive detention laws or to enemy aliens (Article 22(3))."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Local Self-Government","q":"The Eleventh Schedule of the Constitution, relating to the powers of Panchayats under Article 243G, contains how many subjects?","o":["18","29","21","36"],"a":1,"e":"The Eleventh Schedule (73rd Amendment) lists 29 subjects; the Twelfth Schedule (74th Amendment) lists 18 functions for municipalities."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Finance","q":"The Sixteenth Finance Commission, whose recommendations cover the period beginning April 2026, is chaired by:","o":["Vijay Kelkar","Arvind Panagariya","N.K. Singh","Y.V. Reddy"],"a":1,"e":"Dr. Arvind Panagariya heads the Sixteenth Finance Commission constituted under Article 280. N.K. Singh chaired the Fifteenth, Y.V. Reddy the Fourteenth and Vijay Kelkar the Thirteenth. (Verify its latest report details.)"},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Delimitation","q":"Under the proviso to Article 82, as amended in 2001, readjustment of Lok Sabha seats among States is frozen until the first census taken after the year:","o":["2026","2021","2041","2031"],"a":0,"e":"The 84th Amendment (2001) extended the freeze on inter-State reallocation of seats until the first census after 2026. This issue underlies the current debate over delimitation and the southern States."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Judicial Review","q":"Which decision held that judicial review under Articles 226/227 and 32 is part of the basic structure, making tribunal decisions subject to High Court scrutiny?","o":["L. Chandra Kumar v. Union of India (1997)","S.P. Sampath Kumar v. Union of India (1987)","Indira Nehru Gandhi v. Raj Narain (1975)","Kihoto Hollohan v. Zachillhu (1992)"],"a":0,"e":"L. Chandra Kumar overruled Sampath Kumar's exclusion of High Court jurisdiction over tribunals, and held that tribunals act as courts of first instance with appeal to the Division Bench of the High Court."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Elections","q":"In People's Union for Civil Liberties v. Union of India (2013), the Supreme Court directed the Election Commission to provide:","o":["a right to recall elected representatives in mid-term","a 'None of the Above' (NOTA) option on voting machines","compulsory voting for all the registered electors","a complete ban on use of electronic voting machines"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the right to vote includes the right to reject all candidates, protecting secrecy and expression under Article 19(1)(a). Votes cast for NOTA are not counted as valid votes for deciding the winner."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Right to Die","q":"Which decision recognised the validity of advance medical directives (living wills) as part of the right to die with dignity under Article 21?","o":["Aruna Ramachandra Shanbaug v. Union of India (2011)","Common Cause v. Union of India (2018)","Gian Kaur v. State of Punjab (1996)","P. Rathinam v. Union of India (1994)"],"a":1,"e":"The Constitution Bench held that passive euthanasia and living wills are valid; guidelines were simplified in January 2023. Gian Kaur held that the right to life does not include a right to die, while Rathinam was overruled by it."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Custody","q":"In D.K. Basu v. State of West Bengal (1997), as part of safeguards for arrest and detention, the Supreme Court required a medical examination of the detainee every:","o":["48 hours","24 hours","7 days","72 hours"],"a":0,"e":"The guidelines also include an arrest memo attested by a witness, informing a relative of the arrest, and entry in a diary. They address custodial torture and are now reflected in BNSS provisions."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Sexual Offences","q":"Independent Thought v. Union of India (2017) read down Exception 2 to the rape provision of the IPC to hold that intercourse with one's wife is rape if she is:","o":["below 16 years of age","below 21 years of age","below 18 years of age","below 15 years of age"],"a":2,"e":"The Court harmonised the IPC with POCSO and the Prohibition of Child Marriage Act, so that the 'marital exception' cannot cover a wife under 18."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Under Section 69 of the BNS, sexual intercourse by deceitful means, including a false promise of marriage, is punishable with imprisonment up to:","o":["5 years and fine","3 years and fine","7 years and fine","10 years and fine"],"a":3,"e":"Section 69 is a new offence which is distinct from rape. It covers deceitful means such as false promise of marriage, employment or promotion, or concealing identity."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Under Section 106(2) of the BNS, causing death by rash or negligent driving and fleeing without reporting to a police officer or Magistrate is punishable with imprisonment up to:","o":["7 years and fine","2 years and fine","10 years and fine","5 years and fine"],"a":2,"e":"Section 106(1) covers death caused by negligence generally (up to 5 years), with a lower punishment for registered medical practitioners. The hit-and-run limb under 106(2) prompted protests, and the Government kept it in abeyance pending consultation."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"The offence of 'snatching' is separately defined in the BNS under Section:","o":["304","309","310","303"],"a":0,"e":"Section 303 is theft, 304 snatching, 309 robbery and 310 dacoity."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"A 'terrorist act' is defined under the BNS in Section:","o":["147","111","113","152"],"a":2,"e":"Section 113 incorporates terrorism, earlier addressed only by special laws such as UAPA. Section 111 covers organised crime, 147 waging war and 152 acts endangering sovereignty."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Under Section 479 of the BNSS, an undertrial who is a first-time offender is entitled to be released on bail after serving what proportion of the maximum sentence for the offence?","o":["One-third","Two-thirds","One-half","One-fourth"],"a":0,"e":"Other undertrials (not facing death or life imprisonment) become entitled after serving half of the maximum sentence. The Supreme Court in 2024 applied the benefit to pending cases."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Section 356 of the BNSS provides for:","o":["trial in absentia of a proclaimed offender","compounding of non-cognizable offences","plea bargaining in cases involving women","preventive detention of habitual offenders"],"a":0,"e":"The provision allows trial and judgment in the absence of a proclaimed offender who has absconded, after the court records reasons and procedural safeguards are met."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Evidence","q":"Anvar P.V. v. P.K. Basheer (2014) overruled which earlier decision on the admissibility of electronic records?","o":["Kehar Singh v. State (Delhi Administration) (1988)","Selvi v. State of Karnataka (2010)","State (NCT of Delhi) v. Navjot Sandhu (2005)","Nandini Satpathy v. P.L. Dani (1978)"],"a":2,"e":"Anvar held that electronic records as secondary evidence are admissible only with a Section 65B certificate (now Section 63 of the BSA), overruling the Parliament attack case on this point."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Evidence","q":"A confession made by an accused in police custody to a police officer is, as a rule:","o":["admissible only if it is corroborated by other independent evidence","admissible against him if it appears to have been made voluntarily","inadmissible, except to the extent it leads to the discovery of a fact","admissible against him if the accused has signed it before the officer"],"a":2,"e":"The BSA, like the Evidence Act, excludes confessions to police officers unless made in the immediate presence of a Magistrate; only the portion of information leading to discovery is provable."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Common Intention","q":"Under Section 3(5) of the BNS (earlier Section 34 IPC), when a criminal act is done by several persons in furtherance of the common intention of all, each of them is liable:","o":["for that act in the same manner as if it were done by him alone","only if he was physically present at the scene of the offence","only for the part of the act that he personally performed himself","only for abetment of the act done by the principal offender"],"a":0,"e":"Section 3(5) is a rule of joint liability, which requires a pre-arranged plan and participation, and does not create a substantive offence."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Bail","q":"In Sushila Aggarwal v. State (NCT of Delhi) (2020), the Constitution Bench held that anticipatory bail under Section 438 CrPC:","o":["is limited to thirty days from the date on which it was granted","automatically expires when the accused is summoned by the trial court","expires as soon as the police file the charge-sheet before the court","need not be limited to a fixed period; may continue till end of trial"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that protection is not time-bound by default, though courts may impose conditions or limits in appropriate cases. The provision now appears in Section 482 of the BNSS."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Plea Bargaining","q":"Under the BNSS, plea bargaining is available for offences punishable with imprisonment up to:","o":["life imprisonment","ten years","seven years","three years"],"a":2,"e":"Plea bargaining is excluded for offences punishable with death, life imprisonment or imprisonment exceeding seven years, socio-economic offences, and offences against women or children below fourteen."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Private Defence","q":"In exercising the right of private defence of the body, causing death is NOT justified where the only reasonable apprehension is of:","o":["rape","simple hurt","kidnapping or abduction","grievous hurt"],"a":1,"e":"The BNS (like IPC Section 100) extends the right to voluntary death only against assaults that reasonably cause apprehension of death, grievous hurt, rape, unnatural lust, kidnapping, wrongful confinement or acid attack."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Contract","q":"The display of goods with price tags in a self-service shop is, under Pharmaceutical Society of Great Britain v. Boots Cash Chemists (1953):","o":["a unilateral promise by the shopkeeper that binds him to every buyer","an offer by the shopkeeper, accepted when the customer picks up goods","an invitation to treat, with the offer coming from the customer","an acceptance by the shopkeeper, completed when the goods are priced"],"a":2,"e":"The contract is concluded at the cash desk, when the shopkeeper accepts the customer's offer to buy."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Consideration","q":"Under the Indian Contract Act, 1872, consideration may move from:","o":["the promisee alone, and not any other person","the promisor alone, and not any other person","the promisee or any other person","a registered third party alone, not the promisee"],"a":2,"e":"Unlike English law, Section 2(d) uses 'the promisee or any other person', so a stranger to the consideration may sue, though a stranger to the contract cannot (Chinnaya v. Ramayya)."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Offer and Acceptance","q":"A announces a reward for finding his lost child. Y finds the child without knowing of the reward. In Lalman Shukla v. Gauri Dutt, Y:","o":["cannot claim the reward, as acceptance requires knowledge of the offer","can claim a reasonable sum on a quantum meruit basis for his efforts","can claim the reward, since performance of the condition alone suffices","can claim half of the reward as reasonable compensation as a finder"],"a":0,"e":"Acceptance presupposes knowledge of the proposal, so there is no contract where the act is done in ignorance of the offer. The English view in Carlill is distinguished."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Breach","q":"Under Section 39 of the Indian Contract Act, if a party refuses wholly to perform before the time for performance, the other party may:","o":["put an end to the contract, unless he has signalled acquiescence","only claim nominal damages, without being able to end the contract","treat the contract as void for all purposes from the date of refusal","wait until the due date, as the contract cannot be ended before then"],"a":0,"e":"This is the rule of anticipatory breach (Hochster v. De La Tour). The innocent party may accept the repudiation and sue immediately, or wait and keep the contract alive for both parties."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Damages","q":"Under Section 74 of the Indian Contract Act, where a sum is named as a penalty for breach, the aggrieved party is entitled to:","o":["only the actual loss proved, ignoring any amount that was named","reasonable compensation not exceeding the amount named","nominal damages only, in all circumstances of the breach","the full amount named, whatever the actual loss suffered"],"a":1,"e":"Fateh Chand v. Balkishan Dass (1963) held that Section 74 applies whether the clause is liquidated damages or penalty, and that the court awards reasonable compensation up to the stipulated sum."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Partnership","q":"Under Section 69 of the Indian Partnership Act, 1932, an unregistered firm:","o":["cannot be sued by third parties on contracts it has made","has no legal existence as a firm for any purpose at all","cannot sue third parties to enforce contractual rights","is an illegal association and cannot carry on any business"],"a":2,"e":"Non-registration does not invalidate the firm, but bars suits by the firm or its partners against third parties to enforce contractual rights. Set-off in small claims and statutory rights are not affected."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Negotiable Instruments","q":"Under Section 138 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, the payee must issue a demand notice to the drawer of a dishonoured cheque within:","o":["60 days of receiving information of the dishonour","15 days of receiving information of the dishonour","45 days of receiving information of the dishonour","30 days of receiving information of the dishonour"],"a":3,"e":"The drawer then has 15 days from receipt of the notice to pay, failing which the offence is complete and a complaint may be filed within one month."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Defamation","q":"In Subramanian Swamy v. Union of India (2016), the Supreme Court:","o":["upheld the constitutional validity of criminal defamation","read down criminal defamation to cover only public servants","referred criminal defamation to a larger bench without a decision","struck down criminal defamation as a violation of Article 19(1)(a)"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that reputation is part of Article 21 and that the restriction is a reasonable restriction under Article 19(2). The offence continues in Section 356 of the BNS."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Nuisance","q":"In Sturges v. Bridgman (1879), the court held that:","o":["the character of the locality is wholly irrelevant to nuisance","'coming to the nuisance' is no defence to an action in nuisance","a long-standing nuisance cannot be restrained afterwards","prescription can never be acquired for a private nuisance"],"a":1,"e":"The defendant's noisy machinery was an actionable nuisance even though the plaintiff built a consulting room next to it later; the plaintiff's later use did not make the nuisance lawful."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Medical Negligence","q":"In Jacob Mathew v. State of Punjab (2005), the Supreme Court held that criminal liability of a doctor for negligence requires:","o":["any deviation from the standard practice of the profession","vicarious liability of the hospital for its doctors' mistakes","gross negligence or recklessness, not a mere error of judgment","any ordinary negligence causing harm to the patient"],"a":2,"e":"The Court read 'rash or negligent' in the IPC as requiring a very high degree of negligence in the medical context, and laid down that a private complaint needs a credible medical opinion."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Property","q":"A gift of immovable property under Section 123 of the Transfer of Property Act must be made by:","o":["a registered deed signed by the donee and attested by one witness","a registered instrument signed by the donor and attested by two witnesses","delivery of possession alone, without any written instrument being made","an unregistered deed signed by both the donor and the donee together"],"a":1,"e":"Section 122 also requires acceptance by the donee during the donor's lifetime, and a gift of future property is void."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Mortgage","q":"Under Section 60 of the Transfer of Property Act, a provision in a mortgage that prevents the mortgagor from redeeming after the due date is:","o":["valid, if the mortgage is a usufructuary mortgage","valid, if the mortgagor agreed to it expressly","void, as a clog on the equity of redemption","voidable at the option of the mortgagee"],"a":2,"e":"The maxim 'once a mortgage, always a mortgage' underlies the rule that the mortgagor's right to redeem cannot be extinguished by the mortgage deed itself."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Divorce","q":"In Shilpa Sailesh v. Varun Sreenivasan (2023), the Constitution Bench held that the Supreme Court may, under Article 142:","o":["dissolve a marriage only if the High Court first recommends dissolution","dissolve a marriage only if both spouses consent to the dissolution","dissolve a marriage on irretrievable breakdown, within set guidelines","never dissolve a marriage without a statutory ground being established"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that irretrievable breakdown is not a statutory ground but can be a basis for a decree under Article 142, and the mandatory six-month waiting period under Section 13B(2) of the Hindu Marriage Act can be waived in appropriate cases."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Utilitarianism","q":"The principle of 'the greatest happiness of the greatest number' as the basis of legislation is associated with:","o":["John Rawls","Jeremy Bentham","Ronald Dworkin","Immanuel Kant"],"a":1,"e":"Bentham's utilitarianism holds that the aim of law is to maximise pleasure and minimise pain, a view that shaped his criticism of natural law as 'nonsense upon stilts'."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Rights Theory","q":"The idea that individual rights act as 'trumps' over collective goals is associated with:","o":["Joseph Raz","Ronald Dworkin","John Finnis","Lon Fuller"],"a":1,"e":"In Taking Rights Seriously, Dworkin argued that rights are trumps against utilitarian aims, and criticised Hart's positivism for ignoring principles."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Natural Law","q":"The classification of law into eternal, natural, human and divine law is associated with:","o":["Jeremy Bentham","Thomas Hobbes","Hugo Grotius","Thomas Aquinas"],"a":3,"e":"Aquinas, the leading scholastic natural law thinker, held that an unjust human law is not true law but a perversion of law."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Legal Realism","q":"The 'prediction theory' of law, defining law as a prediction of what courts will do in fact, is associated with:","o":["Karl Llewellyn","John Austin","Jerome Frank","Oliver Wendell Holmes"],"a":3,"e":"In 'The Path of the Law', Holmes took the viewpoint of the 'bad man' who cares only about the material consequences of his conduct as predicted by courts."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Rule of Law","q":"Which of the following is NOT a feature of A.V. Dicey's concept of the rule of law?","o":["Constitutional rights as a result of decisions of ordinary courts","Equality of all persons before the ordinary law","A separate system of administrative courts for officials","Supremacy of regular law over arbitrary power"],"a":2,"e":"Dicey rejected the French droit administratif, believing that officials should be subject to the ordinary courts. Modern constitutions, including India's, have a more flexible view with tribunals."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Possession","q":"The two essential elements of possession in jurisprudence are:","o":["locus standi and cause of action, as in suits","mens rea and actus reus, as in criminal cases","dominium directum and dominium utile together","corpus possessionis and animus possidendi"],"a":3,"e":"Corpus is the physical control over a thing, and animus is the intention to hold it as one's own or exclude others."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Corporate Personality","q":"The doctrine of the separate legal personality of a company was established in:","o":["Daimler Co. v. Continental Tyre (1916)","Salomon v. Salomon & Co. Ltd. (1897)","Foss v. Harbottle (1843)","Gilford Motor Co. v. Horne (1933)"],"a":1,"e":"The House of Lords held that a duly incorporated company is a distinct legal person from its members. Gilford and Daimler are cases on lifting the corporate veil, while Foss v. Harbottle states the majority rule."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Sources of Law","q":"Which of the following is NOT an essential requirement of a valid custom?","o":["It must be approved through legislation by the sovereign","It must be continuous and not have been abandoned","It must be reasonable and not opposed to public policy","It must have been observed since time immemorial"],"a":0,"e":"A custom must be ancient, certain, continuous, reasonable and not opposed to statute or public policy; it derives validity from long usage and not from legislative approval."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'actus non facit reum nisi mens sit rea' means:","o":["an act does not make a person guilty unless his mind is also guilty","ignorance of the law is never an excuse for committing an offence","a person is presumed innocent until his guilt is proved in court","there can be no punishment for an act without a prior written law"],"a":0,"e":"The maxim states the general principle that a guilty act and a guilty mind must coincide, subject to statutory exceptions of strict liability."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'volenti non fit injuria' means:","o":["a wrong for which there is no remedy","damage caused without any legal injury","injury caused without any actual damage","to a willing person, no injury is done"],"a":3,"e":"Consent is a defence in torts. The other options describe different ideas: a wrong without remedy, 'injuria sine damno' (legal injury without damage) and 'damnum sine injuria' (damage without legal injury)."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'delegatus non potest delegare' means:","o":["an agent may always appoint a sub-agent at his discretion","a delegate cannot further delegate his delegated power","delegation of legislative power is always void in law","a delegate is liable for the acts of his principal"],"a":1,"e":"The maxim limits sub-delegation in administrative law, unless the parent statute expressly permits it; excessive delegation is a separate ground of invalidity."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Precedent","q":"A judgment rendered 'per incuriam' is one given:","o":["with the consent of both parties to the dispute before it","after hearing only one of the parties to the dispute","in ignorance of a relevant statute or binding precedent","by a bench smaller than the strength required for the case"],"a":2,"e":"Such a decision is not binding as precedent. A bench of lesser strength is a different ground of doubt, for which a reference to a larger bench is the remedy."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"ICJ","q":"Judges of the International Court of Justice are elected by:","o":["the General Assembly alone, by a two-thirds majority of members present","the Economic and Social Council together with the Secretary-General","the General Assembly and the Security Council, each voting independently","the Security Council alone, subject to a veto by a permanent member"],"a":2,"e":"A candidate needs an absolute majority in both organs; the veto does not apply. Judges serve nine-year terms and the Court has 15 members."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Treaties and India","q":"Under Article 253, Parliament may make laws on matters in the State List in order to implement:","o":["only treaties made with neighbouring countries of the Indian Union","any treaty, agreement or decision made at an international conference","only treaties ratified by two-thirds of the States of the Union","only treaties with members of SAARC, BRICS or the Commonwealth"],"a":1,"e":"Article 253 overrides the federal division of legislative powers for treaty implementation; it was used for enacting the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 and the Protection of Human Rights Act, 1993."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Extradition","q":"The principle that an extradited person may be tried only for the offence for which extradition was granted is known as:","o":["the principle of aut dedere aut judicare","the principle of dual criminality","the principle of non-refoulement","the rule of speciality"],"a":3,"e":"Dual criminality means the act must be an offence in both States, non-refoulement bars return to persecution and aut dedere aut judicare means 'extradite or prosecute'."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Diplomatic Law","q":"Under the Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations, 1961, a diplomatic agent's immunity from the criminal jurisdiction of the receiving State is:","o":["denied for serious crimes such as murder","limited to acts done in official capacity","lost automatically if the agent commits an offence","complete, though the sending State may waive it"],"a":3,"e":"Article 31 gives full immunity from criminal jurisdiction; the receiving State's remedy is to declare the agent persona non grata, and waiver must be express."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Environment","q":"The principle of 'no transboundary harm' in international environmental law traces to which arbitration?","o":["Trail Smelter Arbitration (US v. Canada, 1941)","The Barcelona Traction case (Belgium v. Spain, 1970)","The Lotus case (France v. Turkey, 1927)","The Nottebohm case (Liechtenstein v. Guatemala, 1955)"],"a":0,"e":"The tribunal held that no State has the right to use its territory in such a manner as to cause injury by fumes in or to the territory of another."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"ICJ Advisory Opinions","q":"In its July 2024 advisory opinion on the Occupied Palestinian Territory, the ICJ concluded that Israel's continued presence is:","o":["lawful, until a peace treaty is finally concluded","beyond the Court's jurisdiction to examine at all","unlawful and must end as rapidly as possible","lawful, as a security measure pending final status talks"],"a":2,"e":"The Court found it had jurisdiction and discretion to answer the General Assembly's request, and held that the occupation violates the prohibition on acquisition of territory by force and the right to self-determination."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Human Rights","q":"The body that monitors implementation of the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights is:","o":["the Human Rights Committee","the Economic and Social Council","the Human Rights Council","the Committee against Torture"],"a":0,"e":"The Human Rights Committee is a treaty body of 18 experts, which considers State reports and individual communications under the First Optional Protocol. The Human Rights Council is a UN political body."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Climate Law","q":"Article 2 of the Paris Agreement (2015) aims to hold the rise in global average temperature to:","o":["well below 1.5°C, pursuing efforts to limit it to 1°C","well below 2°C, pursuing efforts to limit it to 1.5°C","well below 3°C, pursuing efforts to limit it to 2°C","well below 2.5°C, pursuing efforts to limit it to 2°C"],"a":1,"e":"The target is relative to pre-industrial levels. Parties submit Nationally Determined Contributions every five years."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Humanitarian Law","q":"Common Article 3 of the four Geneva Conventions of 1949 applies to:","o":["only conflicts authorised by the Security Council","only conflicts between two or more States","only occupation of territory by a foreign army","armed conflicts not of an international character"],"a":3,"e":"Common Article 3 sets minimum guarantees of humane treatment for persons not taking active part in hostilities in non-international armed conflicts, and is recognised as customary law."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Trade Marks","q":"The tort of passing off protects:","o":["only marks which the Registrar has declared to be well-known in India","only patents and designs that are registered with the Controller General","only those trade marks that stand registered under the Trade Marks Act, 1999","the goodwill and reputation of a business, even if its mark is unregistered"],"a":3,"e":"Passing off is a common law remedy against misrepresentation causing confusion; Section 27 of the Trade Marks Act preserves it."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Patents","q":"Which of the following is expressly excluded from patentability under Section 3 of the Patents Act, 1970?","o":["a computer programme per se","a new mechanical device with inventive step","a novel chemical process with industrial use","a new method of manufacturing a textile fibre"],"a":0,"e":"Section 3(k) excludes mathematical or business methods, a computer programme per se and algorithms, though programmes with a technical effect may be patentable under CRI Guidelines."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"AI and Copyright","q":"The dispute in ANI Media v. OpenAI before the Delhi High Court primarily raises the question of:","o":["whether training AI models on copyrighted news content infringes copyright","whether AI-generated inventions can be patented under Indian patent law","whether chatbots dilute the famous trade marks owned by news agencies","whether AI firms must register as data fiduciaries under the DPDP Act"],"a":0,"e":"ANI alleges that OpenAI's use of its news content for training and outputs infringes copyright, and the case also tests the jurisdiction over a foreign AI developer and fair dealing under Section 52. (Verify its latest status.)"},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Cyber Law","q":"Cheating by personation using a computer resource is punished under which section of the Information Technology Act, 2000?","o":["Section 66B","Section 66A","Section 66E","Section 66D"],"a":3,"e":"Section 66A (struck down) penalised offensive messages, 66B penalises receiving a stolen computer resource, 66C identity theft, 66D cheating by personation and 66E violation of privacy."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Securities Law","q":"Under the SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations, 2015, an insider is prohibited from trading when in possession of:","o":["audited accounts already filed with the Registrar","any publicly available annual financial statements","unpublished price sensitive information","stock exchange circulars about listing fee schedules"],"a":2,"e":"UPSI is information not generally available which, upon becoming available, is likely to materially affect the price of the securities."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Company Law","q":"The doctrine of indoor management, derived from Royal British Bank v. Turquand, protects:","o":["the company itself from acts that go beyond its objects clause","directors of the company from personal claims made by its creditors","minority shareholders from oppression by the promoters of the company","outsiders dealing in good faith with a company, against internal lapses"],"a":3,"e":"Outsiders are entitled to assume that internal procedures have been complied with, unless they have notice of irregularity or the document is forged."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Real Estate","q":"Under the Real Estate (Regulation and Development) Act, 2016, a promoter must deposit what percentage of amounts collected from allottees in a separate account for construction costs?","o":["60 per cent","70 per cent","50 per cent","90 per cent"],"a":1,"e":"Section 4(2)(l)(D) requires 70% of the amounts realised to be kept in a separate bank account, to be used only for the cost of construction and land."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Taxation","q":"The Goods and Services Tax Council is established under which Article of the Constitution?","o":["Article 270","Article 246A","Article 279A","Article 269A"],"a":2,"e":"Article 279A was inserted by the 101st Amendment (2016). Article 246A gives concurrent power to tax goods and services, and 269A deals with inter-State supplies."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Taxation","q":"In September 2025, the GST Council approved restructuring the rate structure mainly into two standard slabs of:","o":["10% and 20%, with a single flat special rate for luxury goods","5% and 18%, with a special higher rate for sin and luxury goods","12% and 28%, with a separate lower rate for essential goods","5% and 12%, with a complete exemption for all services supplied"],"a":1,"e":"The reform, often called GST 2.0, merged the 12% and 28% slabs into 5% and 18% and introduced a 40% rate for certain demerit goods, effective 22 September 2025. (Verify before the exam.)"},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Insolvency","q":"Under the IBC, a resolution plan must be approved by the Committee of Creditors by a voting share of at least:","o":["90 per cent","51 per cent","75 per cent","66 per cent"],"a":3,"e":"The 2019 amendment reduced the threshold from 75% to 66%. Decisions by the CoC are commercial wisdom, and not ordinarily open to judicial review (K. Sashidhar; Essar Steel)."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Arbitration","q":"An application to set aside an arbitral award under Section 34 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 must be made within three months, which the court may extend by not more than:","o":["15 days","60 days","90 days","30 days"],"a":3,"e":"Section 34(3) allows condonation of only up to 30 days if sufficient cause is shown, and the Limitation Act does not allow any further extension."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Natural Justice","q":"Ridge v. Baldwin (1964) extended the principles of natural justice to:","o":["only judicial and quasi-judicial functions of the courts","administrative decisions affecting the rights of individuals","only proceedings before criminal courts and tribunals","only acts of the legislature, such as delegated legislation"],"a":1,"e":"The House of Lords held that a Chief Constable could not be dismissed without a hearing, reviving natural justice for administrative actions."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Natural Resources","q":"The public trust doctrine was applied to natural resources in India in:","o":["Vellore Citizens' Welfare Forum v. Union of India (1996)","T.N. Godavarman Thirumulpad v. Union of India (1996)","Indian Council for Enviro-Legal Action v. Union of India (1996)","M.C. Mehta v. Kamal Nath (1997)"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that the State is a trustee of natural resources such as rivers, forests and the seashore for the public, and cannot transfer them for private commercial use."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Forests","q":"In T.N. Godavarman Thirumulpad (1996), the Supreme Court held that 'forest' in the Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980 must be understood according to its:","o":["ownership of the land by the State Government or its agency","dictionary meaning, irrespective of ownership or classification","a notification issued by the Central Government for the purpose","entries in the revenue records of the concerned State alone"],"a":1,"e":"The ruling brought all areas answering the description of forest within the Act's protection, and led to a continuing mandamus supervising forest conservation."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Environmental Law","q":"The Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 was enacted to implement the decisions of:","o":["the 1992 Rio Earth Summit on Environment and Development","the 1997 Kyoto Protocol negotiations on greenhouse emissions","the 1972 Stockholm Conference on the Human Environment","the 2015 Paris Conference of the Parties on climate change"],"a":2,"e":"The Act relies on Article 253 and the Stockholm decisions, and was passed in the aftermath of the Bhopal gas tragedy."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Labour Law","q":"Which decision laid down the triple test for deciding whether an enterprise is an 'industry' under the Industrial Disputes Act?","o":["Workmen of Dimakuchi Tea Estate v. Its Management (1958)","Bangalore Water Supply and Sewerage Board v. A. Rajappa (1978)","Kesavananda Bharati Sripadagalvaru v. State of Kerala (1973)","State of Bombay v. Hospital Mazdoor Sabha and Others (1960)"],"a":1,"e":"A seven-judge bench held that systematic activity organised by cooperation between employer and employee for the production or distribution of goods or services is an industry, subject to exclusions such as sovereign functions."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Transgender Rights","q":"In NALSA v. Union of India (2014), the Supreme Court held that transgender persons have the right to:","o":["be recognised as backward only on a medical certificate","be recognised as such only after sex reassignment surgery is done","be recognised only as female persons for all legal purposes","self-identify their gender and be treated as a 'third gender'"],"a":3,"e":"The Court recognised gender identity as part of Articles 14, 15, 16, 19 and 21, directed reservation as socially and educationally backward classes, and rejected a biological test."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Disability Law","q":"Under Section 34 of the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016, the reservation in government posts for persons with benchmark disabilities is:","o":["not less than 3 per cent","not less than 4 per cent","not less than 5 per cent","not less than 6 per cent"],"a":1,"e":"The 2016 Act raised it from 3% under the 1995 Act and recognises 21 disabilities. Section 32 provides for 5% reservation in higher education."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Juvenile Justice","q":"Under the Juvenile Justice Act, 2015, whether a child aged 16 to 18 accused of a heinous offence should be tried as an adult is decided after a preliminary assessment by:","o":["the Sessions Court directly, without the Board","the police officer investigating the offence","the Child Welfare Committee of the district","the Juvenile Justice Board"],"a":3,"e":"The Board assesses the child's mental and physical capacity, ability to understand the consequences and circumstances of the offence, and may transfer the case to the Children's Court."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Domestic Violence","q":"The Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 extends relief to women in:","o":["only relationships involving women who are in paid employment","only a legally wedded marriage solemnised under a personal law","a domestic relationship, including one in the nature of marriage","only relationships between mothers-in-law and daughters-in-law"],"a":2,"e":"D. Velusamy v. D. Patchaiammal (2010) laid down conditions for a live-in relationship to qualify as a relationship in the nature of marriage."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Constitutional Offices","q":"Who was elected the 15th Vice-President of India in September 2025?","o":["M. Venkaiah Naidu","Jagdeep Dhankhar","C.P. Radhakrishnan","Hamid Ansari"],"a":2,"e":"C.P. Radhakrishnan was elected in September 2025 after Jagdeep Dhankhar resigned; the Vice-President is the ex officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha. (Verify before the exam.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Constitution","q":"The Constituent Assembly adopted the Constitution of India on:","o":["9 December 1946","26 January 1950","15 August 1947","26 November 1949"],"a":3,"e":"The Constitution was adopted on 26 November 1949 (now Constitution Day) and most of it came into force on 26 January 1950."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Personal Laws","q":"Which State became the first in independent India to implement a Uniform Civil Code, in January 2025?","o":["Uttarakhand","Assam","Rajasthan","Gujarat"],"a":0,"e":"The Uttarakhand Uniform Civil Code Act, 2024 came into force in January 2025, covering marriage, divorce, succession and live-in relationships, and exempting Scheduled Tribes. Article 44 is a Directive Principle."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Electoral Rolls","q":"In the 2025 litigation on the Special Intensive Revision of Bihar's electoral rolls, the Supreme Court directed the Election Commission to accept Aadhaar as:","o":["proof of identity, though not of citizenship","conclusive proof of citizenship for all voters","proof of residence, but not of identity","proof of date of birth for first-time voters"],"a":0,"e":"The Court asked the ECI to treat Aadhaar as an additional document to establish identity, while citizenship remains a question for the competent authority. (Verify the latest orders.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"RTI and Privacy","q":"The Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 amended which exemption of the RTI Act, 2005, a change widely criticised by transparency advocates?","o":["Section 8(1)(i), on cabinet papers","Section 8(1)(a), on sovereignty and security","Section 8(1)(h), on information impeding investigation","Section 8(1)(j), on personal information"],"a":3,"e":"Section 44(3) of the DPDP Act substitutes Section 8(1)(j) of the RTI Act, removing the public interest override and the proviso that information which cannot be denied to Parliament cannot be denied to a citizen."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Anti-Defection","q":"In July 2025, in a case on defecting legislators in Telangana, the Supreme Court directed the Speaker to decide disqualification petitions ordinarily within:","o":["one year","six months","thirty days","three months"],"a":3,"e":"The Court stressed that a Speaker cannot sit indefinitely on Tenth Schedule petitions, and suggested that Parliament consider an independent tribunal for such decisions. (Verify details.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Advisory Jurisdiction","q":"The advisory opinion of the Supreme Court under Article 143 is:","o":["not binding on the President or Court, but of high persuasive value","binding on the President as well as on all courts under Article 141","binding on the President alone but not on any other court","binding only when delivered by a unanimous bench of judges"],"a":0,"e":"Article 143 opinions are advisory. The Court may decline a reference, and its opinion has a strongly persuasive character but not the binding force of a judgment."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Contempt","q":"Under the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971 (as amended in 2006), truth is a valid defence to a charge of contempt if:","o":["the contemnor is a member of the press","it is bona fide and in the public interest","truth is never a defence to any form of contempt","a bar association certifies the statement as true"],"a":1,"e":"Section 13(b) inserted by the 2006 amendment allows the court to permit justification by truth if it is in public interest and the request is bona fide."}],"b":[{"id":"S1","m":25,"w":450,"q":"The Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 amended Section 8(1)(j) of the RTI Act. Critically examine whether the Act strikes a constitutional balance between the right to privacy and the right to information, with reference to Puttaswamy and Article 19(1)(a).","o":["Constitutional footing: privacy under Article 21 (Puttaswamy, 2017) and the right to know under Article 19(1)(a) (Raj Narain, S.P. Gupta, PUCL).","What changed: Section 44(3) DPDP Act substitutes Section 8(1)(j) of the RTI Act and removes the public interest override.","Arguments for: protects personal data, aligns with proportionality and purpose limitation.","Arguments against: shrinks transparency, risks blanket denial of information on public officials, and may fail the proportionality test (Anuradha Bhasin).","A reasoned conclusion, for example a reinstated public interest test and a clear definition of personal information about public functionaries."]},{"id":"S2","m":25,"w":450,"q":"Article 44 envisages a Uniform Civil Code, and Uttarakhand has enacted one in 2025. Discuss the constitutional questions it raises on federalism, religious freedom under Articles 25 and 26 and gender justice.","o":["Article 44 as a Directive Principle; Shah Bano, Sarla Mudgal, Shayara Bano and Supriyo on personal laws and reform.","Federalism: personal laws are in Entry 5 of List III, so States may legislate, subject to repugnancy under Article 254 with central laws.","Article 25 and 26: essential religious practices test, exemption of Scheduled Tribes, and the Article 25(2)(b) reform power.","Gender justice and privacy concerns, for example the registration of live-in relationships.","Conclusion: a codified framework should be tested on equality, non-arbitrariness and proportionality, ideally after wide consultation."]}]};
